# Question: does a new feature pay for itself in two years, counting the users
# it reaches, how many adopt it and pay for it, and what it costs to build and
# run, and how likely is it to make money?
#
# Run it:
#   ubc scenarios/product/business-case.ubc
# Change an assumption, for example a feature that converts better:
#   ubc --set 'conversion = 5% to 15%' scenarios/product/business-case.ubc
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens net scenarios/product/business-case.ubc
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Amounts are in euros. Each
# factor is a funnel step: being honest about each one beats one big guess.

# Assumptions
reach = 20k to 60k                # users who see the feature in a year
adoption = 10% to 30%             # share of them who try it
conversion = 3% to 10%            # share of those who pay more because of it (upgrade, add-on)
arpu = 150 to 400                 # extra yearly revenue from each paying user
build = 50k to 120k               # building it: engineers × weeks × loaded cost
run = 10k to 30k                  # hosting, support and maintenance a year
years = 2                         # horizon of the business case

# Calculation
yearly_revenue = reach * adoption * conversion * arpu    # the funnel, step by step
net = (yearly_revenue - run) * years - build             # what is left after building and running it

# Result
yearly_revenue
net
P(net > 0)                        # how likely is the feature to make money in two years?
